{"name":"America’s Cup","hex":"#34546d","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cup","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Cupeño."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A concave vessel for drinking, usually made of opaque material (as opposed to a glass) and with a handle.","The contents of said vessel."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cup"}],"entries":[{"title":"America's Cup","blocks":[{"text":"The America's Cup is a sailing competition and the oldest international competition still operating in any sport. America's Cup match races are held between two sailing yachts: one from the yacht club that currently holds the trophy and the other from the yacht club that is challenging for the cup. The winner is awarded the America's Cup trophy, informally known as the Auld Mug. Matches are held several years apart on dates agreed between the defender and the challenger. There is no fixed schedule, but the races have generally been held every three to four years."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/The_America%27s_Cup.jpg/330px-The_America%27s_Cup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/America's_Cup","wikidata":"Q467557"},{"title":"Cup","blocks":[{"text":"A cup is a small container used to hold liquids for drinking, typically with a flattened hemispherical shape and an open \"mouth\", and often with a capacity of about 6–16 US fluid ounces (177–473 ml). Cups may be made of pottery, glass, metal, wood, stone, polystyrene, plastic, lacquerware, or other materials. Normally, a cup is brought in contact with the mouth for drinking, distinguishing it from other tableware and drinkware forms such as jugs; however, a straw and/or lid may also be used. They also often have handles, though many do not, including beakers which have no handle or stem, or small bowl shapes which are very common in Asia."}],"image":